# Jira transitions — confirm before moving a ticket forward

> Cursor mirror of the canonical user-level rule `~/.claude/rules/jira-transition-gate.md`.
> Edit the canonical file first, then this mirror and the committed copy
> `pakfactory.com/.cursor/rules/jira-transition-gate.mdc`.

Moving a ticket forward **summons a human**. BA Acceptance and Request For Approval
put the work in someone's review queue; Done takes it off the board entirely. A
ticket moved before the work is finished sends a reviewer to look at something that
is not there, and it is the *reviewer* who discovers the mistake, not us.

This has happened repeatedly (feedback 2026-09-03). Treat it as the default hazard,
not an edge case.

## Which transitions are gated

| Direction | Examples | Gate |
|---|---|---|
| **Forward — into review or closure** | BA Acceptance, Request For Approval, Done, Deployed, Ready for Release | **CONFIRM FIRST — always** |
| **Forward — by automation** | Merging a PR whose branch or title carries a PROD key into `staging` / `www-new-release` / `dev` → BA Acceptance | **CONFIRM FIRST** — see `jira-ticket-pr-mapping.md` |
| Backward / lateral | To Do, Backlog, reopening | Confirm — it undoes someone's review |
| **Into work** | In Progress, In Development | **No gate.** A statement of intent, reversible, summons nobody |

The point is not friction on every Jira call. It is that a forward move is a claim
**to another person** that the work is ready, and only the user can make that claim.

## Transition IDs vary by issue type — resolve by name

PROD uses a different workflow per issue type, and the same ID means different
statuses (verified 2026-10-01 with `getTransitionsForJiraIssue`):

| Issue type | BA Acceptance | Request For Approval | In Progress |
|---|---|---|---|
| Story / Bug | `4` | `41` | `51` |
| Task | `15` | `7` | `4` |
| Sub-task | `11` | — (no RFA) | `2` |

`4` is BA Acceptance on a Story but **In Progress** on a Task. **Never hardcode an
ID.** Call `getTransitionsForJiraIssue` on the ticket and pick the transition by
`name` immediately before `transitionJiraIssue`. All PROD transitions are global
(reachable from any status), including a status to itself.

## Mandatory gate

Before calling `transitionJiraIssue` in a gated direction:

1. **Never in the same turn as the work.** Finishing a PR and moving the ticket are
   two separate decisions; bundling them is what removes the user's chance to say
   "not yet."
2. **Show the block below.** A bare "shall I move it?" invites a reflexive yes — the
   user cannot judge readiness without the ledger.
3. **Require an explicit yes.** Silence, "thanks", and "good" are not approval.
4. **Never batch.** One confirmation per ticket. Approval to move one ticket is not
   approval to move the others.

Skip the gate only when the user's **same message** names the ticket and the target
state (e.g. "move PROD-2434 to BA Acceptance").

## "Finished" means the acceptance criteria, not the pull request

The bar for a forward move is the ticket's **own acceptance criteria**, each one
marked met, not met, or unverified. Read them. Do not substitute your own summary of
what you built — that is the substitution that produces a premature move.

Three states that are **NOT** finished, and each has looked finished before:

- **A PR that is open.** Not merged is not shipped.
- **Merged but never exercised against the real system.** Passing tests over mocked
  S3, mocked Zoho, or an unseeded database prove the code's shape, not that the
  feature works. Say so explicitly rather than letting "339 tests pass" stand in.
- **Partially delivered.** One criterion out of five is progress, not acceptance.
  If the rest is genuinely out of scope, that is a scope change for the user to
  make — say so and let them decide.

## Confirmation block (copy and fill in)

```
⚠️ Jira transition — needs your confirmation

Ticket:  PROD-#### — <summary>
Move:    <current status> → <target status> (transition id <id>, resolved by name)

Acceptance criteria:
  ✅ <criterion> — <evidence>
  ❌ <criterion> — <what is missing>
  ⚠️ <criterion> — built, NOT verified against the real system

QA checklist: <current in the description? yes / no — update it first (jira-qa-checklist.mdc)>
Merged:      <PRs on the base branch>
Still open:  <PRs not merged>
Verified against reality: <what actually ran outside a test, or "none">
Not done:    <anything left, and whether it is deferred or dropped>

Move it? (yes/no)
```

## Comments are not gated

`addCommentToJiraIssue` is safe and encouraged — a comment recording what shipped,
with PR links and caveats, is how the ticket stays honest **without** claiming the
work is ready for review. When in doubt: **comment, do not transition.** A ticket
with an accurate comment and a stale status costs nothing; a ticket in the wrong
status costs a reviewer's trip.

## Enforcement

In Claude Code, `transitionJiraIssue` is deliberately **NOT** in any
`.claude/settings*.json` allowlist, so the harness prompts for it. Cursor has no such
backstop for MCP calls unless you keep Cursor's tool approval on for the Atlassian MCP —
so in Cursor **this rule is the gate**. Never add `transitionJiraIssue` to any
allowlist or auto-run list, in either tool.

## Skills that transition

These carry the gate in their own steps; keep them in step with this rule:
`finisher` (BA Acceptance), `blog-jira-delivery` (Request For Approval),
`deploy-www-release` (Request For Approval), `wayfinder` (Done).
Each must resolve the transition ID by name — see the table above.

